<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope John Paul II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Karol Józef Wojtyła, John Paul II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope John Paul II, born Karol Józef Wojtyła in Wadowice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, was head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and sovereign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> from 16 October 1978 until his death in 2005, the first non-Italian pope since Adrian VI in the 16th century. Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> from 1964, he visited 129 countries as pope and canonised 483 saints. His 1979 visit to Poland contributed to the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> movement in 1980, and he has been credited with helping to end communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He survived being shot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Mehmet Ali</a> Ağca in 1981, and has been criticised over his response to the sexual abuse of children by priests. He was canonised in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii.jpg" alt="Pope John Paul II" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregorini Demetrio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADAMELLO_-_PAPA_-_Giovanni_Paolo_II_-_panoramio_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karol Józef Wojtyła was the youngest of three children of Karol Wojtyła, an ethnic Pole, and Emilia Kaczorowska, a schoolteacher. His mother died in 1929, when he was eight. In his youth he worked occasionally as a stage actor, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he took work in a quarry to avoid being sent to a German forced labour camp. Adam Stefan Sapieha, the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, encouraged him to study theology and ordained him on 1 November 1946. On 4 July 1958 Pope Pius XII appointed him auxiliary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Kraków, and at 38 he became the youngest bishop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Pope John Paul I died following only 33 days as pope, a second conclave opened on 14 October 1978, split between Cardinal Giuseppe Siri and Cardinal Giovanni Benelli. Cardinal Franz König suggested Wojtyła as a compromise, and he won on the eighth ballot on 16 October, taking his predecessor's name. He wrote 14 encyclicals, completed a reform of the Church's legal system and the Roman Curia, and saw the 1983 Code of Canon Law and the Catechism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> promulgated. He beatified 1,344 people and canonised 483 saints.</p><p>On 13 May 1981 he was shot in the abdomen in St. Peter's Square by Mehmet Ali Ağca, lost nearly three-quarters of his blood and underwent five hours of surgery. As pope he made public apologies for more than 100 wrongdoings, among them the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> (31 October 1992) and the silence of many Catholics during the Holocaust (1998).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 2 April 2005 and was succeeded by Pope Benedict XVI. Pope Francis canonised him on 27 April 2014, alongside Pope John XXIII, after his beatification on 1 May 2011. He has been credited with helping to end communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and under him the Church greatly expanded its influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The sexual abuse of children by priests has also brought criticism of him, for allegedly not acting against it or being insufficiently harsh with it, both as archbishop in the Polish People's Republic and as pope.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope John Paul II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church’s ban on Copernican books was relaxed only gradually: the general prohibition was dropped in 1758, and Copernicus’s book and Galileo’s Dialogue left the Index of Prohibited Books in 1835; in 1992 <b>Pope John Paul II</b> acknowledged the errors of Galileo’s judges.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>That same year, 1979, <b>Pope John Paul II</b>, a native of Poland, visited the country; a figure Poles could identify with, beyond the regime’s reach, he was later named by Lech Wałęsa as a significant factor in Solidarity’s creation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope John Paul II</b> unveiled the restoration on 8 April 1994.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>…and 1970s it was sung at Catholic folk masses and as a hymn in Protestant churches; in 1997 <b>Pope John Paul II</b> drew on it before some 300,000 young Italian Catholics at a church congress where Dylan performed, and in 2007 Pope Benedict XVI wrote that he had…</blockquote><a href="/songs/blowin-in-the-wind">Blowin' in the Wind</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Paul II</b>, the first non-Italian pope in over four centuries, died in the Vatican.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The move reflected close cooperation with <b>Pope John Paul II</b> during the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Holy Father John Paul II Family Home in Wadowice, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.88,19.49&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Holy Father John Paul II Family Home in Wadowice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4997 428 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope John Paul II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5447 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5447 698h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.88,19.49&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
